            for p in range(q-1, l-1, -1):
                if crosses(ids[p], rq):
                    if ids[p] > bad[rq]: bad[rq]=ids[p]
                    break
        return
    mid=(l+r)//2
    cdq(l,mid)
    # contributions left [l,mid) to right [mid,r)
    process_cross(l,mid,r)
    cdq(mid,r)

YOUR TASK: Complete this task: Implement the full Python program (no fragments) using the forbidden-pair characterization: convert each person to l=min(S,T)-1, r=max(S,T)-1, sign=0 if S<T else 1; a pair conflicts iff same l with different (r,sign), same r with different (l,sign), or same-sign strict crossing l_i<l_j<r_i<r_j or l_j<l_i<r_j<r_i. Compute bad[j]=max conflicting i<j. Endpoint conflicts: one forward pass with top-two latest distinct keys per left endpoint and per right endpoint (key=(other endpoint,sign)). Same-sign crossing: for each sign separately, scan i=M..1 and keep alive future intervals of that sign in two coordinate structures over 0..N-1: A keyed by L with a heap of encoded (N-1-R, idx) and a segment tree storing max R; B keyed by R with a heap of encoded (L, idx) and a segment tree storing min L. For current i, decompose the coordinate range [L_i+1, R_i-1] once into segment-tree canonical nodes; for each node, if its max R > R_i, descend iteratively to all leaves in that node whose max R > R_i and for each alive interval j with R_j>R_i set bad[j]=max(bad[j],i), mark j dead (lazy deletion in both heaps), and update the tree; if its min L < L_i, do the analogous descent for B with L_j<L_i. Use strict inequalities, encoded heap keys with a SHIFT=M.bit_length() so idx=key&MASK, lazy cleanup of dead heap tops, and pull ancestor paths after leaf/node updates. Then insert i into both structures. After both signs, build prefix max pref[j]=max(pref[j-1],bad[j]) and answer query [L,R] Yes iff pref[R]<L. Run the two samples and ensure exact output.